<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bouches-de-l'Elbe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1811–1814</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bouches-de-l'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> created in 1811, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> annexed the north German coast to enforce his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of British trade. Named after the mouths of the Elbe, it had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> as its capital and also included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and neighbouring lands. It ended with the French withdrawal from Hamburg in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bouches-de-l-elbe.jpg" alt="Bouches-de-l'Elbe" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Departement-Elbm%C3%BCndungen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
